---
// One statewide page, not one per county. The FAQ carries FAQPage schema, and
// the same schema on five URLs makes them compete with each other for the same
// query instead of one of them winning it. Nothing in the answers is
// county-specific — the statutes and the carrier rules are the same in Pinellas
// and Orange — so there is nothing four pages could have said that this one
// cannot.
//
// The page still has to lead to an inspector, and a statewide page cannot know
// which county the reader is in, so it offers the county list rather than a
// single button. That list sits under the lead copy rather than under the FAQ:
// someone who already knows they need a 4-point should not have to read eight
// questions to reach an inspector.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { counties } from '../lib/counties';

const live = counties.filter((c) => c.status === 'live');
---

<Layout
  title="4-Point and Wind Mitigation Inspections in Florida | Verified Home Inspector"
  description="What Florida insurers want at renewal: a 4-point on an older home and a wind mitigation for storm discounts, both from a DBPR-licensed home inspector."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="county-tag">Insurance renewals</div>
      <h1>Switching your homeowner's insurance company?</h1>
      <p style="color:var(--navy-soft);font-weight:500;margin-top:10px;max-width:68ch;">
        Start with your insurance agent. Ask them to shop other carriers for the same house, and
        they will tell you what the new carrier needs. In almost every case that means a current
        4-point inspection, and often a wind mitigation as well, which is what earns the
        storm-resistance discounts. Both are performed by a home inspector licensed by the
        Florida DBPR, and both are yours to schedule.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What follows is deliberately not a second set of definitions. The FAQ
       below already answers what a 4-point is, what a wind mitigation is, and
       how long each stays valid. These are the things a homeowner in the
       middle of switching carriers actually has to do, in the order they have
       to do them, which nothing else on the site says. -->
  <section class="section" id="what-to-expect" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="legal" style="max-width:68ch;">
        <h2>How it usually goes</h2>
        <p>
          Your agent shops carriers and comes back with what the quoting carrier wants. You book
          the inspections and you pay for them. The inspector sends you the report, and you
          forward it to your agent, who submits it with the application. The carrier reads the
          reports before it binds the policy rather than after, so nothing moves until they are
          in hand.
        </p>

        <h2>Book both in the same visit</h2>
        <p>
          A 4-point and a wind mitigation are separate reports, but the same licensed inspector
          can do both on one visit, and most will. Ask for both when you book, even if your agent
          has only mentioned the 4-point so far. The wind mitigation is the one that earns
          discounts rather than the one that satisfies a requirement, so leaving it out costs you
          money rather than time.
        </p>

        <h2>What the inspector needs to reach</h2>
        <p>
          Plan on access to the electrical panel, the water heater, the air handler, and the
          attic hatch, and clear a path around all four sides of the house. If you have permit
          records or receipts for a roof replacement or a re-pipe, find them before the visit.
          The age of the roof covering, and the permit date behind it, are what several wind
          mitigation credits are scored against.
        </p>

        <h2>If the 4-point turns something up</h2>
        <p>
          A few findings stop a policy rather than simply lowering a score. The usual ones are a
          Federal Pacific or Zinsco electrical panel, aluminum branch wiring, polybutylene supply
          plumbing, a roof with little remaining life, and any active leak. None of those are
          statutory the way the roof-age rule is — they are underwriting decisions and they vary
          by carrier — but they are common enough to be worth knowing before a renewal deadline
          rather than a week after it. A carrier will generally want the repair done and the
          report re-issued before it binds.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The way to an inspector sits directly under the copy that explains why
       someone needs one, rather than below the FAQ. A reader who already knows
       they need a 4-point should not have to scroll past eight questions
       answering a question they no longer have. -->
  <section class="section" id="find-an-inspector" style="padding-top:8px;">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Find a licensed inspector</h2>
        <div class="note">Every listing verified against the Florida DBPR record</div>
      </div>
      <p style="color:var(--slate);max-width:68ch;margin-top:0;">
        Requesting an inspector sends your details to that inspector and to nobody else. We never
        pass the same homeowner to several inspectors, and we never sell contact information.
      </p>
      <!-- A dropdown rather than a row of buttons: Florida has 67 counties and
           this list only grows. Live counties only — a county with no data yet
           has a "coming soon" page and no #all-inspectors section to land on,
           so offering it here would be a dead end from a button that promises
           inspectors. -->
      <div class="county-picker">
        <label class="visually-hidden" for="inspectorCountySelect">Choose your county</label>
        <select id="inspectorCountySelect">
          <option value="">Choose your county…</option>
          {live.map((c) => (
            <option value={c.slug}>{c.name}{c.metro ? ` · ${c.metro}` : ''}</option>
          ))}
        </select>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq />

  <script>
    // Its own id: the header carries a #countySelect of its own, and two
    // elements sharing an id would leave one of them unwired.
    const select = document.getElementById('inspectorCountySelect') as HTMLSelectElement | null;
    select?.addEventListener('change', () => {
      if (!select.value) return;
      window.location.href = `/fl/${select.value}/#all-inspectors`;
    });
  </script>
</Layout>
